In this lesson we'll look at how to find the rule for an inverse function. The idea is actually pretty simple, and it works in three steps. First, write your function out with y on one side and an expression in x on the other. That just turns the function rule into something easier to play with. Next, swap x and y. This is the key move, because finding an inverse means reversing the machine, so the input becomes the output and the output becomes the input. Finally, solve for y, and whatever expression you end up with is your inverse function. A couple of things to remember. The domain and the range swap over, so whatever x values the original function could take, those become the range of the inverse, and vice versa. It's also a great idea to check your answer by feeding the result into the original function, or the other way around. If you get back to where you started, you've nailed it. Now let's see it in action.